  "query_preview": "/FRIDAY NIGHT THROUGH WEDNESDAY/...\nIssued at 112 PM CST Wed Nov 25 2026\n\nAfter the brief period of ridging on Friday, another 500 MB \ntrough will approach from the west and traverse Iowa Friday \nnight, followed by a deeper and stronger wave around Saturday \nnight. As the first wave moves overhead there will be a shot of \nmodest mid/upper level forcing, however, a lack of moisture will\ninhibit precipitation reaching the surface, and have carried \nonly a slight chance of light rain/snow in our far northeastern \ncounties around Friday night. When the stronger and deeper \nsystem passes by later Saturday/Saturday night, it will be \nattended by a developing surface cyclone moving from Oklahoma to\nnear the Missouri/Arkansas border. A large zone of deeper \nmoisture and frontogenetical forcing around the northern \nhemisphere of the surface low, and beneath the upper forcing \nassociated with the 500 MB trough, will generate a large swath \nof precipitation across Missouri and into at least southern \nIowa. It is difficult to determine at this range where the \nnorthern edge of this precipitation field will lie, but \nsomewhere across the northern half of Iowa is the most probable \nsolution. As such, will maintain high (70-90%) rain chances \nacross central and southern Iowa on Saturday night, with more \nmoderate (50-60%) chances farther north. Forecast temperatures \nand soundings do support rain as the predominant precipitation \ntype in our area, with overnight lows in the mid to upper 30s \nsouth, and in the north where temperatures may be cold enough to\nsupport snow the precipitation chances and expected amounts are\nlower. Therefore, at this time impacts from the late Saturday-\nSaturday night system appear minimal in our area, but we will be\nclosely monitoring for any chances in storm track or thermal \nprofiles over the next few days.\n\nOn Sunday and Monday the weather will be dry and quiet, with \nweak ridging in wake of the Saturday night trough, then sometime\naround Tuesday it appears another trough will move across the \nregion but it will be considerably less focused or robust than \nthe preceding weekend system.",
